{
  "_about": [
    "Brand rules for grouping trims (vpa/trims.py). Read when the data is read; nothing here changes the database.",
    "Per make: 'codes' finds generation codes written in the trim by pattern (BMW chassis codes); 'series' lists codes with the years they were sold, and a code only counts when the car's year fits (Ford's XT and XL are trims too); 'qualifiers' follow a code and belong to it (FG MkII, G20 LCI II); 'body' fills a missing body type from the generation code; 'same_as' borrows another make's rules.",
    "Years are model years, inclusive, with one year's slack either side for series codes. null = still on sale.",
    "'years' (per model): generations by model year, [name, first, last] - the MY code is used before the listing year. Overlapping ranges are deliberate where both generations were sold: a car in the overlap with no MY code is not guessed: it is named with both (quattro B8/B9). A generation from a year table is added to the trim name ('quattro B9'); a car with no trim takes the generation as its name ('C3').",
    "'badges' (per model): a pattern in the trim that decides the generation outright (LS460 is always XF40), checked before 'years'.",
    "'era_words' (per model): words that are the era's name rather than a trim in those generations (a 1966 Corvette 'Sting Ray' is the C2 itself), dropped from the trim there.",
    "DRAFTED 12/10/2026 from general knowledge, Australian-market model years - check them. Edit this file and every past car regroups on the next page load."
  ],
